// Health Career Photography lessons 6–10: shooting in health care settings, editing and printing,
// writing the narrative, assembling and uploading the portfolio, and the Round Two presentation.
const RULES=['HOSA Health Career Photography 2026–27 guidelines and rating sheets','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/HCPhoto-26-27-8.16-FINAL.pdf'];
const BLS_OOH=['U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics: Occupational Outlook Handbook—healthcare occupations','https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/home.htm'];
const EHC=['Explore Health Careers','https://explorehealthcareers.org/'];
const ISO=['CDC: isolation precautions guideline','https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/isolation-precautions/index.html'];
const UPLOAD=['HOSA: Competitive event digital uploads','https://hosa.org/competitive-event-digital-uploads/'];
const COPY=['U.S. Copyright Office: What is copyright?','https://www.copyright.gov/what-is-copyright/'];
const q=(p,a,b,c,d,e)=>[p,[a,b,c,d],e];
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const order=(title,steps)=>({t:'w',kind:'order',title,steps});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const HP6={title:'Shooting in health care settings',hook:'Clinics, pharmacies and hospitals are busy, sensitive places where patient care always comes first. Photographing there requires planning, infection control awareness and quiet professionalism. This lesson helps you get great photos while being a respectful, safe guest in someone’s workplace.',objectives:['Plan a photo session around patient care.','Follow infection control and safety rules on site.','Work unobtrusively while capturing strong images.','Use simulation and demonstrations effectively.'],sections:[
['Planning the session',[
'Before arriving, confirm the **time, location, contact person** and **areas you may photograph**. Ask what tasks the professional will perform and plan two or three possible shots. Bring charged batteries, extra storage, a cleaning cloth, signed forms (and blanks) and a notebook.',
'Ask for a time when the professional can safely pause or demonstrate tasks—often during training, slower periods or in a skills lab. Keep sessions short; 30 to 60 minutes is common.',
"Send a brief reminder message the day before the shoot to confirm the time and location. Reminders reduce last-minute cancellations and show professionalism.",
order('Before the shoot',['Confirm time, place and contact','Ask which areas are allowed','Plan two or three shot ideas','Charge equipment and clear storage','Pack forms and a notebook','Review the facility’s rules'])]],
['Infection control and safety',[
'Follow the facility’s **infection control** rules: perform **hand hygiene** before and after entering care areas, wear required **PPE** (such as a mask), avoid touching equipment or surfaces unnecessarily and stay out of sterile or isolation areas unless invited. Clean your camera or phone before and after the visit.',
'Be aware of safety hazards: wet floors, moving equipment, sharps containers and radiation areas. Never interfere with patient care or emergency responses.',
'If a patient situation arises, step back immediately.',
"Ask the professional about any equipment that should not be touched or photographed, such as items with patient information or sensitive security areas.",
"Wear closed-toe shoes and avoid dangling jewelry or loose clothing, which can be safety hazards near equipment and may not meet facility dress expectations.",
sort('Safe and appropriate on site?',['Appropriate','Not appropriate'],[['Using hand sanitizer before entering a care area',0],['Entering an isolation room to get a better angle',1],['Cleaning your phone after the visit',0],['Touching sterile instrument trays',1],['Stepping back when an emergency occurs',0]])]],
['Working unobtrusively',[
'Move quietly, stay out of walkways and keep a respectful distance. Turn off shutter sounds if possible and avoid flash. Explain to anyone nearby who you are if asked, and always defer to staff.',
'Patience produces better photos: watch the workflow, anticipate moments and wait for the professional to be naturally absorbed in a task.',
'A professional who feels comfortable with you will act more naturally on camera.',
"Position yourself where you can see the professional’s face and hands without blocking movement. A corner of the room or a spot beside the work area often works well.",
"Ask the professional to repeat a task if you missed the moment, but only when it does not interfere with patient care. Demonstrations in training areas make repetition easier.",
chk('Why turn off shutter sounds and avoid flash?',['To avoid disturbing patients and staff','To make photos darker','Because HIPAA requires it','To save battery only'],'Be unobtrusive.')]],
['Simulation and demonstrations',[
'**Simulation labs**, training rooms and mannequins are excellent for photography: they avoid patient privacy issues and let professionals demonstrate tasks deliberately. Ask the professional to perform the skill as they would with a patient, using correct technique and equipment.',
'Make simulated scenes look **authentic**: real uniforms and badges (with no patient information), appropriate equipment and realistic positioning. Avoid staged scenes that misrepresent how the job is done.',
"Even in simulation, make sure safety steps such as gloves, hand hygiene and patient identification are shown correctly. Judges with health care experience will notice errors.",
"Thank the professional at the end of the session and tell them when you expect to share the final photo. Many appreciate seeing how their work was portrayed.",
match('Setting and photo opportunity',[['Simulation lab','Nurse practicing on a high-fidelity mannequin'],['Pharmacy counter','Pharmacist counseling a consenting volunteer'],['Therapy gym','Therapist guiding a balance exercise'],['Lab bench','Scientist pipetting samples'],['Ambulance bay','Paramedic checking equipment'],['Training classroom','Instructor demonstrating a skill']])]],
['Variety and coverage',[
'For each professional, capture **variety**: wide environmental shots, medium shots of the task and close-ups of hands and equipment. Take many photos—dozens or more—so you have strong options. Shoot both **landscape and portrait** orientations, since either is allowed for the 8″ × 10″ print.',
'Leave a little space around the subject so cropping to 8″ × 10″ proportions does not cut off important parts.',
"Review photos briefly on site to confirm you have strong options before leaving. Returning for a reshoot is much harder than taking extra shots during the first visit.",
"If the setting allows, photograph the same task from two or three positions. Comparing angles later helps you choose the image that best shows the career and the professional’s skill.",
ex('Planning coverage','Plan shots for a physical therapist session (scenario invented for practice).',['Wide: therapist and volunteer in the therapy gym','Medium: therapist guiding a balance exercise','Close-up: therapist’s hands steadying the volunteer’s elbow','Detail: therapist recording progress on a tablet (no screen visible)','Both orientations for each'],'Varied coverage gives many options for the final photo (scenario invented for practice).')]],
['Taking notes on site',[
'After or between shots, note **what task** was happening, **why** it matters, the **equipment** used, the professional’s **title and setting** and quotes from your conversation. Record photo file numbers next to notes so you can match them later.',
'These notes support the narrative (responsibilities, education, benefits and challenges) and Round Two’s description of the photo, where judges look for evidence you took the photo yourself.',
"After the shoot, back up photos to a second location, such as a computer or cloud storage, so a lost or broken phone does not cost you the project.",
sort('Useful note for the narrative or presentation?',['Useful','Not useful'],[['“Pt. balance training after knee surgery; uses gait belt for safety.”',0],['“Room was cold.”',1],['“PT said doctorate (DPT) took 3 years after college.”',0],['“Parking lot was full.”',1],['“Hardest part: patients losing motivation.”',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('On-site judgment',{a:{text:'During your shoot, a patient emergency begins nearby. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Stop photographing, step out of the way and follow staff instructions','b','Correct.',true],['Photograph the emergency for drama','b','Never interfere or photograph without consent.',false]]},b:{text:'Later, the professional offers to demonstrate on a mannequin in the training room. Is that a good option?',choices:[['Yes—it allows accurate, privacy-safe photos of the skill','c','Correct.',true],['No—mannequins are not allowed','c','Simulation is a strong, safe option.',false]]},c:{text:'You capture strong, respectful images.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Plan sessions with confirmed times, allowed areas, shot ideas, charged equipment, forms and a notebook; keep sessions short and scheduled around patient care.','Follow infection control (hand hygiene, PPE, no sterile or isolation areas), avoid hazards, never interfere with care and work quietly without flash; simulation labs and mannequins give authentic, privacy-safe images.','Capture variety (wide, medium, close-up, both orientations), leave room for 8″ × 10″ cropping and take detailed notes linked to file numbers for the narrative and presentation.'],terms:[['Simulation lab','A training space with mannequins for practicing care.'],['Unobtrusive','Not drawing attention or interfering.'],['Coverage','A variety of shots of the same subject and task.']],mcqs:[q('What should you do before entering a care area?','Perform hand hygiene','Use flash','Touch equipment','Enter isolation rooms','Infection control.'),q('If an emergency occurs nearby, you should:','Step back and follow staff instructions','Photograph it','Ask questions loudly','Ignore it','Priority.'),q('Why use simulation labs?','They avoid privacy issues and allow accurate demonstrations','They are required','They make photos blurry','They replace permission forms','Strategy.'),q('Which shot shows the work environment?','Wide environmental shot','Extreme close-up','Blurred shot','Selfie','Coverage.'),q('May the 8″ × 10″ photo be portrait or landscape?','Either','Only landscape','Only portrait','Only square','Rule.'),q('Why leave space around the subject?','So cropping does not cut off important parts','To add graphics later','To include yourself','To save storage','Cropping.'),q('Which note is most useful?','The task, its purpose and equipment used','The weather','Parking availability','Lunch menu','Notes.'),q('How should you move on site?','Quietly and out of walkways','Quickly through patient areas','Wherever you want','Using flash constantly','Conduct.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would plan and conduct a safe, respectful photo session in a health care setting.',rubric:[['Planning',['confirm','areas','shot ideas','batteries','forms','notebook']],['Safety and infection control',['hand hygiene','ppe','isolation','sterile','emergency','patient care']],['Approach',['unobtrusive','quiet','flash','simulation','mannequin','authentic']],['Coverage and notes',['wide','medium','close-up','orientation','crop','notes','file numbers']]],model:'Before photographing a physical therapist, I would confirm the time, location and contact person, ask which areas I may photograph and plan two or three shot ideas, such as guiding a balance exercise and recording progress. I would charge my phone, clear storage, clean the lens and pack signed permission forms, blank copies and a notebook, and I would review the clinic’s rules. On site, I would perform hand hygiene before entering care areas, wear any required mask, avoid touching equipment, stay out of isolation or sterile areas and step back immediately if any patient situation required staff attention. I would work quietly, stay out of walkways, turn off shutter sounds and avoid flash, watching the workflow and waiting for natural moments of concentration. If patient privacy made real care difficult to photograph, I would ask the therapist to demonstrate on a consenting volunteer or mannequin in a training area, using correct technique, uniforms and equipment so the scene looks authentic. I would capture variety—a wide shot of the therapy gym, medium shots of the exercise and close-ups of the therapist’s hands steadying the volunteer—in both landscape and portrait orientations, leaving space for 8-by-10-inch cropping and taking many shots. Finally, I would note what task was happening, why it mattered, the equipment and quotes about education, benefits and challenges, linking them to photo file numbers for my narrative and presentation.'},sources:[RULES,ISO]};

export const HP7={title:'Editing and printing ethically',hook:'Editing can turn a good photo into a great one—but in this event, the rules draw a clear line. You may edit and crop, but you may not add graphics, backgrounds, collages or other elements. Ethical editing improves what you captured without changing what really happened, and careful printing makes your 8″ × 10″ photos look their best.',objectives:['Distinguish allowed and prohibited edits.','Make basic corrections that improve quality.','Crop to 8″ × 10″ without losing important content.','Print high-quality photos for the portfolio.'],sections:[
['What the rules allow',[
'Competitors may use software to **edit and enhance** photos. **Editing and cropping are permitted**, but **adding graphics, backgrounds, photo collages and other elements is not permitted**.',
'In practice, allowed edits include adjusting **brightness, contrast, exposure, color and white balance**, **straightening**, **cropping** and converting to **black and white**. Prohibited changes include adding text, stickers, borders with graphics, replacement backgrounds, combining multiple photos or inserting objects.',
"Removing small dust spots or sensor marks is a minor correction, but avoid removing objects that are part of the scene. When in doubt, ask your advisor whether an edit is acceptable.",
sort('Allowed edit?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['Adjusting brightness and contrast',0],['Adding a hospital logo graphic',1],['Cropping to remove a distracting edge',0],['Replacing the background with a stock image',1],['Converting to black and white',0],['Combining three photos into a collage',1]])]],
['Basic corrections',[
'Start with **global** adjustments: **exposure** (overall brightness), **contrast** (difference between lights and darks), **highlights and shadows** (recovering detail), **white balance** (removing color casts) and **saturation or vibrance** (color intensity—use lightly). **Straighten** tilted horizons and vertical lines.',
'Keep edits **natural**—skin tones should look real and the scene should look like what you saw. Over-editing looks artificial and can lower exposure and originality scores.',
"Edit all three photos with a similar style so they look consistent as a set, such as similar contrast and natural color, while respecting differences in lighting.",
"Use editing tools gently. Small adjustments to brightness and contrast are usually enough; heavy filters can make health care scenes look unrealistic.",
order('Editing workflow',['Duplicate the original file','Straighten and crop','Adjust exposure and contrast','Recover highlights and shadows','Correct white balance','Fine-tune color and sharpen lightly'])]],
['Cropping to 8″ × 10″',[
'An 8″ × 10″ print has a **4:5 aspect ratio**, while most phone and camera photos are **3:4** or **2:3**. Cropping to 4:5 removes part of the image, so check that the professional, their hands and key equipment remain fully visible.',
'Use the crop tool with a 4:5 (or 8×10) preset. Cropping can also **improve composition**—placing the subject on a rule-of-thirds line or removing distracting edges.',
"If cropping to 4:5 would cut off important content, consider using the portrait orientation, which fits many vertical scenes more naturally.",
"Sharpen lightly, if at all. Excessive sharpening creates halos around edges that look obvious in an 8″ × 10″ print.",
ex('Checking resolution for printing','A photo is 4000 × 3000 pixels (invented for practice). Is it enough for an 8″ × 10″ print at 300 dpi?',['300 dpi at 8″ × 10″ needs 2400 × 3000 pixels','Cropping 4000 × 3000 to 4:5 gives about 2400 × 3000 pixels','That meets 300 dpi','Avoid cropping further'],'Yes—about 2400 × 3000 pixels supports a sharp 8″ × 10″ print (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Honesty in editing',[
'Ethical health care photography represents reality. Do not remove or add objects in ways that change what happened, alter a professional’s appearance misleadingly or make a task look different from how it is actually performed.',
'Cropping out an identifiable bystander or a distracting edge is acceptable; digitally erasing a patient’s face or inserting equipment is not (and adding elements is prohibited). If privacy cannot be protected by cropping, choose a different photo or reshoot.',
"Keep the original, unedited files. If judges or advisors ever question an image, the originals show exactly what you captured.",
chk('A patient’s face is visible at the edge of your best photo. What is the appropriate fix?',['Crop it out if possible, or choose or reshoot a different photo','Paste a sticker over the face','Add a blurred graphic','Leave it in'],'Crop or reshoot—no added elements.')]],
['Printing the photos',[
'Print each photo at **8″ × 10″** on quality photo paper through a photo lab, pharmacy photo center, school media center or online service. Check a **test print** for color and brightness—screens are often brighter than prints, so you may need slightly lighter edits.',
'Handle prints by the edges, store them flat and place each photo **(only the photo)** in a **clear sheet protector**, writing the **photo number** (#1, #2, #3) in the **bottom right corner** of the photo or sheet protector.',
"Allow time for printing delays and reprints. Order prints at least a few weeks before the upload deadline so photos can be scanned or included in the PDF on time.",
"Check prints in different lighting, such as daylight and indoor light, before inserting them in the portfolio. Judges may view them under bright conference lighting.",
match('Printing step and detail',[['Size','8″ × 10″'],['Orientation','Landscape or portrait'],['Paper','Quality photo paper'],['Test print','Check color and brightness'],['Sheet protector','Photo only, clear'],['Numbering','#1–#3, bottom right corner']])]],
['Choosing the final three',[
'Review all photos for each professional and select the one that best shows **focus of occupation**, **originality**, **exposure and framing** and a clear story. Ask your advisor or a classmate which photo tells the career most clearly.',
'Make sure the three final photos clearly show **three different fields** and look consistent in quality as a set.',
"Print a small proof sheet of your top choices for each career and compare them side by side. Seeing them printed often makes the strongest choice obvious.",
sort('Strong final photo choice?',['Strong choice','Weak choice'],[['Sharp, well-lit, professional clearly at work',0],['Slightly blurry but “cool” angle',1],['Career instantly recognizable',0],['Professional facing away with task hidden',1],['Natural colors and clean background',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Editing decisions',{a:{text:'You want to add the words “Respiratory Therapist” across your photo. (Scenario invented for practice.) Is that allowed?',choices:[['No—adding text or graphics is not permitted; the title page lists career titles','b','Correct.',true],['Yes, it helps judges','b','Added elements are prohibited.',false]]},b:{text:'Your print looks darker than the screen. What do you do?',choices:[['Lighten the edit slightly and make a new test print','c','Correct.',true],['Submit it as is','c','Check and correct before final printing.',false]]},c:{text:'Your edited, printed photos are ready for the portfolio.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Editing and cropping are allowed (exposure, contrast, white balance, straightening, black and white); adding graphics, text, backgrounds, collages or other elements is not.','Keep edits natural; crop to 8″ × 10″ (4:5) without cutting off key content and check resolution (about 2400 × 3000 pixels for 300 dpi); edit honestly and crop or reshoot for privacy instead of adding elements.','Print at 8″ × 10″ on photo paper after a test print; place only the photo in a clear sheet protector numbered #1–#3 in the bottom right; select the strongest three in three different fields.'],terms:[['Cropping','Trimming the edges of a photo.'],['Aspect ratio','The proportional relationship between width and height.'],['Test print','A trial print to check color and brightness.']],mcqs:[q('Which edit is allowed?','Adjusting brightness','Adding a logo','Replacing the background','Creating a collage','Rule.'),q('Which is NOT allowed?','Adding text across the photo','Cropping','Straightening','Black and white conversion','Rule.'),q('What aspect ratio is an 8″ × 10″ print?','4:5','16:9','1:1','3:2','Proportion.'),q('About how many pixels does an 8″ × 10″ print need at 300 dpi?','2400 × 3000','800 × 1000','300 × 300','10,000 × 10,000','Resolution.'),q('Where is the photo number written?','Bottom right corner of the photo or sheet protector','On the back only','In the narrative only','Nowhere','Rule.'),q('What goes in the clear sheet protector?','Only the photo','Photo and permission forms','The narrative','The title page','Rule.'),q('Why make a test print?','Prints often look darker than screens','It is required by HIPAA','To add graphics','To change size','Printing.'),q('A visible patient face can be fixed by:','Cropping or reshooting','A sticker graphic','A blurred overlay','Ignoring it','Ethics.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would edit and print your three photos within the event rules.',rubric:[['Rules',['editing','cropping','permitted','graphics','backgrounds','collages','not permitted']],['Corrections',['exposure','contrast','white balance','straighten','natural','black and white']],['Cropping and printing',['8″ × 10″','4:5','resolution','300 dpi','test print','photo paper']],['Portfolio prep and ethics',['sheet protector','number','bottom right','honest','privacy','select']]],model:'The rules allow me to edit and crop my photos but not to add graphics, text, backgrounds, collages or other elements. I would always duplicate the original file first, then straighten and crop, adjust exposure and contrast, recover highlights and shadows, correct white balance for clinic lighting and fine-tune color lightly so skin tones and the scene look natural—or convert to black and white if it strengthens the image. Because an 8-by-10-inch print has a 4:5 aspect ratio, I would use a 4:5 crop preset, make sure the professional’s face, hands and equipment remain fully visible and use the crop to place the subject on a rule-of-thirds line, confirming the cropped image still has about 2400 by 3000 pixels for a sharp 300 dpi print. I would edit honestly, never adding or removing objects to change what happened; if a patient’s face appeared at the edge, I would crop it out or choose or reshoot a different photo rather than adding a blur or sticker. I would order a test print at a photo lab, lighten the edit if the print looked darker than the screen and then print the final photos at 8 by 10 inches on quality photo paper. Finally, I would select the strongest photo for each professional with my advisor’s input, place only each photo in a clear sheet protector and write its number—#1, #2 or #3—in the bottom right corner.'},sources:[RULES,COPY]};

export const HP8={title:'Writing the narrative descriptions',hook:'Each photo is paired with a one-page narrative worth 10 points—and narrative formatting earns 5 more. Judges want a description that brings the profession to life: job responsibilities, education and training, benefits and challenges and what you found interesting, with no spelling errors. The narrative proves that you learned about each career, not just photographed it.',objectives:['Include all four required narrative elements.','Write vivid, accurate career descriptions.','Apply the narrative formatting rules.','Proofread to eliminate errors.'],sections:[
['Required content',[
'Each one-page narrative explains: **(a)** the professional’s **job responsibilities**, **(b)** **required education and training**, **(c)** **benefits and challenges** and **(d)** any other **specific information from this experience** you found interesting.',
'The top score describes the experience in detail, brings the profession to life, covers points a–d thoroughly and has **no spelling errors**. Scores drop with vague information and with each spelling error (1–2 errors, 3–4 errors, 5+ errors).',
"Write the narrative soon after each shoot while details and impressions are fresh. Waiting weeks can make the writing vague and less personal.",
"Each narrative should feel unique to its career. Avoid reusing the same sentences across all three; judges notice repetitive writing.",
flip('Narrative elements',[['Responsibilities','Specific tasks the professional performs'],['Education and training','Degrees, credentials and time required'],['Benefits','Rewards of the career'],['Challenges','Difficulties of the career'],['Experience','What you found interesting'],['Spelling','Zero errors for full points']])]],
['Gathering accurate information',[
'Combine your **interview notes** with reliable sources such as the **BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook** and **Explore Health Careers** for education requirements and job duties. Verify credentials and degree levels—for example, physical therapists need a **Doctor of Physical Therapy** degree and a state license.',
'Use the professional’s own words for personal insights and reliable sources for general facts.',
"Check that education information matches the specific career shown. A pharmacy technician and a pharmacist, for example, have very different education requirements.",
"Include one or two specific numbers where helpful, such as the years of education required, but keep the focus on the professional and your experience rather than statistics.",
match('Information and best source',[['Typical daily tasks','Interview with the professional'],['Degree required','BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook'],['License or certification','Professional association or state board'],['Personal rewards','Interview quotes'],['Career pathway','Explore Health Careers'],['Interesting moment','Your own experience at the shoot']])]],
['Writing vividly',[
'Open with a **hook** that connects to the photo: “Every morning, Ms. Patel’s first patient is someone learning to walk again.” Then describe responsibilities with **specific action verbs**, explain education clearly, present benefits and challenges honestly and end with what **you** learned or found surprising.',
'Write in a professional but engaging voice. Avoid generic sentences like “This job helps people.” Show instead how the professional helps.',
"Use the professional’s name and title only with their permission. Many competitors refer to the professional by title alone, such as “the respiratory therapist.”",
"Vary sentence length and structure to keep the narrative engaging. A mix of short, punchy sentences and longer descriptive ones holds the reader’s attention.",
sort('Vivid or vague?',['Vivid','Vague'],[['“She adjusts each patient’s exercises based on their balance that day.”',0],['“This job is important.”',1],['“He double-checks every dose against the prescription before handing it over.”',0],['“They help people a lot.”',1],['“I was surprised that sonographers often comfort anxious parents.”',0]])]],
['Formatting rules',[
'Narratives must be **one-sided** and **typed** in **12-point Arial**, **double-spaced**, with **1-inch margins** on **8½″ × 11″** paper. Include a **header** with your **last name** and the **event name** at the **top left** and the **page number** at the **top right**. Each narrative is **one page**.',
'Because double-spacing limits length, plan about **250–300 words** per narrative and edit to fit one page. Formatting is worth 5 points in the overall portfolio section.',
"Save the narrative template with the header and page number already set, so each new narrative starts with correct formatting.",
"Avoid copying text from career websites. The narrative should be in your own words, combining researched facts with your personal experience.",
ex('Planning a one-page narrative','Allocate about 280 words across the four elements.',['Hook and responsibilities: about 100 words','Education and training: about 60 words','Benefits and challenges: about 70 words','Interesting experience: about 50 words','Total: about 280 words, double-spaced on one page'],'A balanced narrative that fits one page.')]],
['Proofreading',[
'Spelling errors directly reduce the narrative score. Use spell-check, then proofread **aloud** and slowly, checking career titles, medical terms and the professional’s name and facility. Have a teacher or classmate review each narrative.',
'Check that the header, page number, font and spacing are correct on every narrative page, and that each narrative follows its photo in the portfolio.',
"Read the narrative while looking at the photo. The words and image should support each other, and the narrative should mention what the photo shows.",
"Ask the professional to review the narrative for accuracy if they are willing. A quick check from an expert can catch errors in job details or terminology.",
order('Proofreading process',['Run spell-check','Read aloud slowly','Verify medical terms and titles','Check header, page number and formatting','Ask another reader to review','Make final corrections'])]],
['Connecting narrative and presentation',[
'Your narrative and Round Two presentation should tell the same story, but the presentation adds energy and detail. Use the narrative to prepare: understanding of the profession, description of the photo and **how the career fits into the health care system**.',
'Note in your narrative or notes at least **three connections** to the health care system—for example, who refers patients, who the professional collaborates with and how their work affects outcomes.',
"Use the narrative as the foundation for your notecards, choosing two or three key facts and one memorable detail for each photo.",
"Practice summarizing each narrative aloud in about 30 seconds. This helps you remember key facts without needing to read during the presentation.",
chk('Which detail supports “connection to the health care system”?',['“Physicians refer patients to her after surgery, and she reports progress to the care team.”','“She likes coffee.”','“The clinic has blue walls.”','“She drives a car.”'],'Shows how the career fits.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Narrative review',{a:{text:'Your pharmacist narrative covers duties and education but not challenges. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is missing?',choices:[['Benefits and challenges—required element (c)','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing','b','All four elements are required.',false]]},b:{text:'Your narrative runs onto a second page. What do you do?',choices:[['Edit to one page while keeping all four elements','c','Correct.',true],['Shrink the font to 10 point','c','The rules require 12-point Arial.',false]]},c:{text:'Your narrative is complete, vivid and correctly formatted.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Each one-page narrative covers job responsibilities, education and training, benefits and challenges and interesting details from your experience (10 points per photo; spelling errors reduce scores).','Combine interview notes with reliable sources (BLS OOH, Explore Health Careers); write vividly with hooks, action verbs and specific examples rather than generic statements.','Format: one-sided, typed, 12-point Arial, double-spaced, 1-inch margins, 8½″ × 11″, last name and event top left, page number top right (5 points); plan about 250–300 words, proofread aloud and note connections to the health care system.'],terms:[['Narrative description','A one-page written explanation of the career in each photo.'],['Header','Text at the top of the page with last name and event name.'],['Action verb','A verb describing a specific task, such as “adjusts” or “verifies.”']],mcqs:[q('Which is a required narrative element?','Benefits and challenges','Salary history of the competitor','The facility’s address','The weather','Rule.'),q('What font is required?','12-point Arial','10-point Times','14-point Calibri','Any font','Format.'),q('What spacing is required?','Double-spaced','Single-spaced','1.5-spaced','Triple-spaced','Format.'),q('Where does the page number go?','Top right','Bottom center','Top left','Nowhere','Format.'),q('How long is each narrative?','One page','Two pages','Half a page','Five pages','Rule.'),q('Which sentence is vivid?','“He double-checks every dose against the prescription.”','“This job is important.”','“They help people.”','“It is a good career.”','Writing.'),q('What reduces the narrative score?','Spelling errors','Specific details','Accurate facts','Clear structure','Rubric.'),q('Which source verifies education requirements?','BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook','A social media post','A cartoon','A guess','Accuracy.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would write a one-page narrative for one of your photos that earns full points.',rubric:[['Content',['responsibilities','education','training','benefits','challenges','interesting']],['Sources and accuracy',['interview','bls','explore health careers','verify','license']],['Writing quality',['hook','vivid','action verbs','specific','professional','learned']],['Formatting and proofreading',['12','arial','double-spaced','1-inch','header','page number','one page','spelling']]],model:'For my photo of a physical therapist, I would write a one-page narrative of about 280 words covering all four required elements. I would open with a hook tied to the photo—“Every morning, Ms. Patel’s first patient is someone learning to walk again”—then describe her responsibilities with specific action verbs: evaluating patients’ strength and balance after surgery or injury, designing individualized exercise plans, guiding exercises safely with a gait belt, educating patients and families and documenting progress for the care team. Using the BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook and Explore Health Careers to confirm facts, I would explain that physical therapists need a bachelor’s degree, a Doctor of Physical Therapy degree that typically takes about three years and a state license after passing a national exam. For benefits and challenges, I would include her quote about the reward of watching patients regain independence and the challenges of physically demanding work and keeping patients motivated. I would end with what I found interesting, such as how she adjusts exercises each day based on the patient’s balance, and note connections to the health care system, like referrals from surgeons and reports to the care team, for Round Two. The narrative would be one-sided and typed in 12-point Arial, double-spaced with 1-inch margins on 8½-by-11-inch paper, with my last name and the event name at the top left and the page number at the top right. I would run spell-check, read it aloud, verify every medical term and have my advisor review it so it has zero spelling errors.'},sources:[RULES,BLS_OOH,EHC]};

export const HP9={title:'Assembling, uploading and displaying the portfolio',hook:'Your portfolio is your entire Round One score—and your companion through display time and Round Two. Easy points come from getting the details right: the official HOSA portfolio, a complete title page, pages in the correct order, numbered photos, permission forms in place and one combined PDF uploaded on time.',objectives:['Assemble the 13-page portfolio in the required order.','Complete the title page correctly.','Create and upload one clear combined PDF.','Prepare for the Required Project Display Time.'],sections:[
['The portfolio order',[
'Use an **official HOSA portfolio or notebook from the HOSA Store**. Pages in order: **1** title page; **2** Photo #1 (8″ × 10″ in a clear sheet protector, numbered bottom right); **3** Photo #1 narrative; **4–5** Photo #1 facility and subject permission forms (not in sheet protectors); **6–9** Photo #2, narrative and forms; **10–13** Photo #3, narrative and forms.',
'Photos must be numbered **1, 2, 3** and presented in that order in Round Two. Correct numbering earns 5 points.',
"Use sheet protectors that are clear and free of scratches so judges can see the photos well, and check that photos sit straight inside them.",
"Insert pages carefully so the photo faces forward when the portfolio is opened. Judges should see each photo upright without turning the notebook.",
order('Portfolio pages',['Page 1: title page','Page 2: Photo #1','Page 3: Photo #1 narrative','Pages 4–5: Photo #1 permission forms','Pages 6–9: Photo #2 set','Pages 10–13: Photo #3 set'])]],
['The title page',[
'The **title page** (one page only) includes the **event name**, **competitor’s name**, **HOSA division**, **chapter number**, **school name** and **chartered association**, and lists **all three career titles photographed**, centered and neatly presented. Creative design or pictures may be used but do not affect the score.',
'The title page is worth 5 points—check that every required item is present and spelled correctly.',
"A simple, clean title page design helps judges find required information quickly, even though decorations do not affect the score.",
"Use a clear, readable font for the title page and match it with the narrative style for a consistent look throughout the portfolio.",
sort('Required on the title page?',['Required','Not required'],[['Event name',0],['Competitor’s name',0],['All three career titles',0],['Chapter number',0],['Your GPA',1],['Professionals’ home addresses',1]])]],
['Creating the PDF',[
'Upload the portfolio as **one combined PDF** by **May 15 at midnight EST**—no exceptions. Scan or photograph each page at high resolution, or export digital files, then combine them in order into one PDF. Use the original high-resolution image files for the photo pages so judges see sharp images.',
'It is your responsibility to make sure the photos are **legible, clear and large enough** to view effectively. Open the finished PDF and zoom in on each photo, narrative and form before uploading. If your state used the HOSA upload system, upload again for ILC.',
"Name the PDF clearly, such as with your name and event, and keep a copy saved on your device and in cloud storage.",
"Compare the PDF page order against the required order one final time before uploading. A single misplaced page can cost formatting or organization points.",
ex('Building the PDF','Plan the combined PDF (one possible approach).',['Export title page and narratives from your word processor as PDFs','Insert the high-resolution photo files as full pages','Scan signed permission forms at 300 dpi','Combine all pages in portfolio order','Zoom in to check every page'],'One clear, complete PDF in the correct order.')]],
['Pre-judging and display time',[
'Portfolios are **pre-judged in early June**; finalists are determined then, but results are **released after the Required Project Display Time**. All competitors must attend display time, standing with their **physical portfolios** and sharing experiences with delegates; missing it costs **15 points** from Round Two.',
'Bring the **hard copy** of your portfolio to ILC for display time and Round Two—it is the **only** thing you may show judges.',
"Because some competitors may participate only in display time, treat it as an important part of the event, not a formality. It is a chance to share your work with many delegates.",
flip('ILC portfolio facts',[['Pre-judging','Early June'],['Results','Released after display time'],['Display time','Required; −15 if missed'],['Bring','Physical portfolio'],['Show judges','Only the portfolio'],['Pickup','Take portfolio when instructed']])]],
['Talking with visitors',[
'Prepare a short explanation of each photo for visitors: the career, the task shown and one interesting fact you learned. Display time is excellent practice for Round Two.',
'Handle the portfolio carefully, keep sheet protectors clean and be ready to answer questions such as how you arranged the shoots and what surprised you.',
"Bring a soft cloth to wipe fingerprints from sheet protectors before display time and Round Two.",
"Practice a 20-second explanation for each photo before ILC. Delegates may visit only briefly, so short, clear explanations help you share your work with more people.",
chk('What is the best preparation for display time conversations?',['A short explanation of each photo’s career and story','Silence','Reading the narratives aloud word for word','Discussing other events'],'Practice for Round Two.')]],
['Final checklist',[
'Before uploading and traveling, confirm: official HOSA portfolio; title page complete; three photos 8″ × 10″, numbered 1–3, in sheet protectors; three narratives correctly formatted with no errors; facility and subject forms behind each narrative; three different fields; one combined PDF uploaded and checked.',
'Keep a digital backup and protect the physical portfolio during travel.',
"Ask your advisor to check the portfolio against the rating sheet before you upload. A second reviewer often catches missing forms or numbering errors.",
"Pack the portfolio in a sturdy bag or case for travel and keep it with you rather than in checked luggage, so it arrives safely at ILC.",
sort('Ready for submission?',['Ready','Fix needed'],[['Forms behind each narrative, outside sheet protectors',0],['Photo #2 missing its number',1],['Narrative in 12-point Arial, double-spaced',0],['PDF uploaded as three separate files',1],['Title page lists all three career titles',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Portfolio check',{a:{text:'Your scanned PDF makes photos look small and blurry. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Insert the original high-resolution photo files as full pages and recheck','b','Correct.',true],['Upload it anyway','b','Photos must be clear for pre-judging.',false]]},b:{text:'You put permission forms inside the photo sheet protectors. Is that correct?',choices:[['No—only the photo goes in the sheet protector; forms go behind the narrative','c','Correct.',true],['Yes','c','Forms should not be in sheet protectors.',false]]},c:{text:'Your portfolio and PDF are complete and correct.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Use the official HOSA portfolio; pages: title page, then for each photo the 8″ × 10″ numbered photo (in a sheet protector), narrative and facility and subject permission forms (not in protectors).','The title page lists event, name, division, chapter number, school, chartered association and all three career titles (one page, 5 points); upload one clear combined PDF by May 15 using high-resolution photo files and check every page.','Pre-judging is in early June with results after display time; attend display time with the physical portfolio (−15 if missed), the only item shown to judges; use a final checklist and keep a backup.'],terms:[['Combined PDF','A single file containing every portfolio page in order.'],['Title page','The first portfolio page with competitor details and career titles.'],['Hard copy','The physical printed portfolio.']],mcqs:[q('Which page comes first?','Title page','Photo #1','Permission forms','Narrative #1','Order.'),q('What follows each photo?','Its narrative, then permission forms','The title page','Another photo','Nothing','Order.'),q('How many pages is the full portfolio?','13','3','20','8','Structure.'),q('How must the portfolio be uploaded?','As one combined PDF','As separate images','As a link','On paper','Rule.'),q('What may be shown to judges in Round Two?','Only the portfolio','A slideshow','Props','A video','Rule.'),q('When are results released?','After display time','Before pre-judging','On May 15','Never','Timing.'),q('Which belongs on the title page?','All three career titles','Your GPA','Patients’ names','Facility phone numbers','Rule.'),q('Whose responsibility is photo clarity in the PDF?','The competitor’s','HOSA’s','The judges’','The printer’s','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would assemble, upload and display your Health Career Photography portfolio.',rubric:[['Order',['title page','photo','narrative','permission forms','13','sheet protector','number']],['Title page',['event','name','division','chapter','school','chartered association','career titles']],['PDF',['one combined pdf','high-resolution','legible','check','may 15','upload again']],['Display',['display time','15 points','physical portfolio','only','visitors','results after']]],model:'I would assemble my portfolio in an official HOSA notebook from the HOSA Store. Page 1 would be the title page with the event name, my name, HOSA division, chapter number, school name, chartered association and all three career titles—physical therapist, sonographer and pharmacist—centered and neat. Page 2 would be Photo #1, an 8-by-10-inch print alone in a clear sheet protector with “#1” in the bottom right corner; page 3 its one-page narrative; and pages 4 and 5 its facility and subject permission forms, not in sheet protectors. Pages 6 through 9 and 10 through 13 would repeat this pattern for Photos #2 and #3, with copies of facility forms if photos share a location. To create the PDF, I would export the title page and narratives from my word processor, insert the original high-resolution photo files as full pages, scan the signed forms clearly and combine everything in order into one PDF, zooming in on every page to confirm legibility before uploading well before May 15 at midnight EST, and uploading again for ILC if my state used the system. Because pre-judging happens in early June and results are released after display time, I would attend the Required Project Display Time with my physical portfolio to avoid the 15-point penalty, sharing a short explanation of each photo with visitors. I would keep a digital backup, protect the portfolio during travel and bring it to Round Two, since it is the only item I may show judges.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOAD]};

export const HP10={title:'The Round Two presentation',hook:'You have about a minute and a half per photo to show judges what each career is, what is happening in the image and how the career fits into health care. Round Two awards 30 points per photo plus delivery points—and judges watch for evidence that you took the photo and truly understand the profession. Preparation turns three photos into three compelling stories.',objectives:['Structure a five-minute presentation of three photos.','Demonstrate understanding of each profession.','Describe each photo vividly as its photographer.','Give at least three connections to the health care system for each career.'],sections:[
['Presentation rules',[
'You present each photo **in order (1, 2, 3)**, holding the portfolio or photos as you present. You have a **maximum of five minutes**—about **one and a half minutes per photo**—to present the picture, **describe the profession**, **explain what is happening** and explain **how the career fits into the health care system**. A flash card shows one minute remaining, and you are stopped at five minutes.',
'Notecards are allowed (electronic notes are not shown). The **portfolio is the only thing** you may show judges. Take the portfolio with you when instructed.',
"Practice holding the portfolio open to each photo smoothly. Fumbling with pages wastes time and distracts from your message.",
"Begin with a short introduction stating the three careers you explored, so judges know the structure before you present the first photo.",
order('Presenting one photo',['Hold up the photo and name the career','Describe what is happening in the photo','Explain key responsibilities and requirements','Give three ways the career connects to the health care system','Transition to the next photo'])]],
['Understanding of the profession',[
'Judges score whether you show a **clear understanding of the profession** depicted and its **job requirements** (10 points per photo). Explain responsibilities, education and credentials and work settings concisely, using correct terminology.',
'Avoid simply reading your narrative; speak naturally, highlighting the most important facts.',
"Mention one requirement or credential for each career, such as a degree or license. Specific requirements show judges a clear understanding of the profession.",
"Use correct titles and terms, such as “registered respiratory therapist” or “Doctor of Physical Therapy,” to show precise knowledge of each profession.",
chk('Which statement best shows understanding of a sonographer’s profession?',['“Sonographers use ultrasound to create images that help physicians diagnose conditions, and most complete an accredited associate degree and earn certification.”','“Sonographers work in hospitals.”','“It is a medical job.”','“They take pictures.”'],'Specific duties and requirements.')]],
['Describing the photo',[
'The top score goes to competitors who **bring the photo to life**—vividly illustrating its story with details about **what is happening and why**—making it **evident the competitor took the photo**. Share behind-the-scenes details: how you arranged the shoot, what the professional was doing, why you chose the angle and what happened just before or after.',
'Weak descriptions leave judges with more questions than answers or suggest you did not take the photo.',
"Share a brief anecdote from each shoot. A short story about what happened makes it evident that you took the photo and makes your description memorable.",
"Point to specific parts of the photo as you describe them—the professional’s hands, the equipment or the setting—so judges follow your story visually.",
"Avoid vague phrases like “it was cool.” Instead, describe specific sensations or observations—the hum of the ultrasound machine or the quiet focus in the room.",
sort('Brings the photo to life?',['Brings it to life','Weak description'],[['“I waited for the moment she steadied his elbow as he took his first step without the bars.”',0],['“This is a physical therapist.”',1],['“I chose a low angle to show how carefully he checks each dose.”',0],['“Here is a picture.”',1],['“Right after this, the patient laughed and asked to try again.”',0]])]],
['Connections to the health care system',[
'The top score for **connection to the health care system** requires **three or more thorough examples** of how the career fits (10 points per photo). Consider: **who refers** patients to this professional, **whom they collaborate** with, **how their work affects** diagnosis, treatment or recovery, **where** they work in the system and **how** they contribute to safety, cost or access.',
'Prepare at least three connections for each career in your notes.',
"Vary your connections across the three photos so you show breadth—referrals, teamwork, diagnosis, treatment, safety and cost are all good categories.",
"Connections can also include the professional’s role in prevention and education, such as a pharmacist counseling patients on medication safety or a therapist teaching home exercises.",
match('Career and system connection',[['Physical therapist','Receives referrals from surgeons after joint replacement'],['Pharmacist','Checks prescriptions for interactions to prevent errors'],['Sonographer','Provides images radiologists use for diagnosis'],['Phlebotomist','Collects samples for laboratory testing'],['Respiratory therapist','Works with ICU physicians and nurses on ventilator care'],['Medical lab scientist','Reports results that guide treatment decisions']])]],
['Timing and structure',[
'Plan about **10 seconds** for a brief introduction, **about 90 seconds per photo** and **about 10–20 seconds** for a short closing reflection on what you learned. Practice with a timer and adjust so each photo receives equal attention.',
'Use transitions such as “My second photo takes us from the therapy gym to the pharmacy.”',
"Rehearse in front of family or classmates and ask them which photo they remember best and why. Use their feedback to strengthen the weaker descriptions.",
"If judges ask questions at the end, answer briefly and refer back to the photos. Questions are a chance to show even deeper understanding.",
ex('Timing plan','Plan a five-minute presentation (one possible plan).',['Introduction: 0:10','Photo #1 (physical therapist): 1:30','Photo #2 (sonographer): 1:30','Photo #3 (pharmacist): 1:30','Closing reflection: 0:15','Total: 4:55'],'Equal time per photo with a short margin.')]],
['Delivery',[
'Delivery items—**voice**, **stage presence** and **diction**—are worth 5 points each. Speak clearly, make eye contact with judges, hold photos steadily at a comfortable height so judges can see them and avoid fillers. Show enthusiasm for each career.',
'Dress professionally for bonus points, and bring the **official HOSA portfolio** (5 points).',
"End your presentation by briefly explaining how the project shaped your own career interests. A personal closing leaves judges with a strong final impression.",
"Arrive early to your appointment, review your notecards and take a calm breath. A relaxed start helps you speak clearly and confidently from the first photo.",
sort('Helps or hurts delivery?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Holding the photo steady toward the judges',0],['Covering the photo with your hand',1],['Eye contact while describing the photo',0],['Reading the narrative word for word',1],['Enthusiastic, clear voice',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Presentation day',{a:{text:'In practice, you spend three minutes on Photo #1. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Trim to about 90 seconds so each photo gets equal time','b','Correct.',true],['Skip Photo #3','b','All photos are scored equally.',false]]},b:{text:'You have only one example of how the pharmacist fits into the health care system. How do you improve?',choices:[['Add at least two more, such as working with physicians and counseling patients','c','Correct.',true],['One example is enough','c','Three or more earn top points.',false]]},c:{text:'Your presentation is balanced, vivid and connected to health care.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Present photos in order 1–3 within five minutes (about 90 seconds each), holding the portfolio—the only item shown—with a card at one minute remaining.','For each photo, show understanding of the profession and its requirements, bring the photo to life with behind-the-scenes details that show you took it and give three or more thorough connections to the health care system (10 points each per photo).','Plan timing with transitions and a brief closing; deliver with clear voice, eye contact, steady photo handling and enthusiasm; use the official HOSA portfolio (5 points).'],terms:[['Connection to the health care system','How a career relates to other professionals, patients and outcomes.'],['Behind-the-scenes detail','Information about how and why a photo was taken.'],['Transition','A phrase that moves smoothly from one photo to the next.']],mcqs:[q('About how long should each photo be presented?','About 90 seconds','About 10 seconds','About 4 minutes','About 30 seconds','Timing.'),q('In what order are photos presented?','1, 2, 3','Any order','3, 2, 1','Judges choose','Rule.'),q('How many connections to the health care system earn top points?','3 or more','1','2','None','Rubric.'),q('What may be shown to judges?','Only the portfolio','A slideshow','A video','Props','Rule.'),q('Which description shows the competitor took the photo?','Behind-the-scenes details of the moment','“This is a photo.”','Reading the title','Silence','Rubric.'),q('How many points is each Round Two content item worth per photo?','10','5','15','20','Rubric.'),q('Which delivery habit hurts?','Covering the photo with your hand','Eye contact','Clear voice','Enthusiasm','Delivery.'),q('When is the one-minute card shown?','With one minute remaining','At the start','After each photo','Never','Timing.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline how you would present one of your photos in about 90 seconds to earn full points.',rubric:[['Profession',['understanding','responsibilities','education','credentials','terminology']],['Photo description',['what is happening','why','details','angle','took the photo','story']],['System connections',['three','referrals','collaborate','diagnosis','treatment','safety']],['Delivery and timing',['90 seconds','order','eye contact','hold','transition','enthusiasm']]],model:'Holding up Photo #2, I would say: “This is Mr. Rivera, a diagnostic medical sonographer at our regional hospital.” In about 20 seconds, I would show understanding of the profession: sonographers use high-frequency sound waves to create images of organs, blood flow and developing babies, most complete an accredited associate degree program with clinical rotations and earn certification through a national registry exam. In about 35 seconds, I would bring the photo to life: “I took this photo in the hospital’s training room, where he demonstrated a cardiac scan on a volunteer classmate. I chose a low angle so the ultrasound screen frames his face as he adjusts the probe, and just after this shot, he pointed out the heart valves opening and closing.” Then, in about 30 seconds, I would give three connections to the health care system: physicians order the scans he performs, radiologists and cardiologists interpret his images to diagnose conditions such as heart valve disease, and his work helps avoid more invasive or costly tests while guiding treatment decisions. I would close with a transition: “My final photo takes us from imaging to the pharmacy.” Throughout, I would hold the photo steady at chest height toward the judges, make eye contact, speak clearly and enthusiastically and use notecards only for key phrases, keeping each photo to about 90 seconds within the five-minute limit.'},sources:[RULES,BLS_OOH]};
